# Build artefacts.
/bin/
/dist/

# The built binary, anchored to the repository root.
#
# This must stay anchored. A bare `cli-zapp` pattern matches any file or
# directory of that name at any depth — including `cmd/cli-zapp/` — which
# is how the program's entry point went untracked and the committed tree
# stopped building. A gitignore pattern that is meant for one artefact must
# say where it is.
#/cli-zapp

# makepkg's scratch trees.
#
# These appear only if makepkg is run from inside the checkout rather than from the
# copy that scripts/mkarch.sh makes. They are ignored *and* mkarch.sh avoids
# creating them, because makepkg leaves $pkgdir mode 0711 when a build fails
# partway — and `go build ./...` walks this whole tree, so one failed package build
# would otherwise leave the Go toolchain unable to read its own module.
/packaging/arch/pkg/
/packaging/arch/src/
/packaging/arch/*.pkg.tar.*
/packaging/arch/*.sig

# Test and coverage output.
coverage.out
coverage.html
*.test
*.prof

# Backups produced by scripts/backup.sh. They contain a full git bundle and must
# never be committed or uploaded alongside the source they came from.
*.tar.zst
*.tar.zst.sha256
*.tar.gz.sha256

# Dependencies are NOT vendored. The comment that used to claim otherwise was
# wrong and cost a reader time: there is no vendor/ directory, and go.sum pins the
# module graph, which is what makes the build reproducible. Run `go mod vendor`
# explicitly if a future change wants that.

# Local configuration and secrets. The linked session lives in the system
# keyring, but a stray config must never be committed.
/cli-zapp.toml
/.env
*.key

# Runtime data. Nothing here belongs in version control: these are per-user
# session databases and logs, and a committed one is a credential leak.
*.db
*.db-journal
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.log

# Editor and OS noise.
.DS_Store
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/